    Run the tests in CI, and refuse to start without basic auth
    
    The 26 offline tests guard real production failures -- the missing-markdown-mirror
    bug that broke four consecutive weekly refresh runs, per-article failure
    isolation, the circuit breaker's exit code -- and nothing was executing them.
    .github/workflows/tests.yml now does, on every push and pull request.
    
    It installs with --no-dev, the same set app.py and refresh_kb.py get in
    production, so it doubles as a guard on the dependency manifest: the tqdm gap
    fixed in f9a6c45 is exactly the class of bug this would have caught. A second
    step imports the entry points the suites don't cover (visualize_embeddings, both
    scraper modules), which otherwise only fail on deploy or during the weekly run.
    
    The job is given no secrets deliberately. Both suites setdefault() placeholder
    credentials before importing and never touch the network, so if one ever starts
    reaching out it fails in CI instead of quietly hitting the live database.
    
    app.py's launch() passed auth=[(os.getenv("APP_USERNAME"), os.getenv("APP_PASSWORD"))]
    with no validation, so a missing variable became auth=[(None, None)] and the app
    stood up anyway with a login form in an unclear state. For an internal tool on a
    public URL, that's the one failure mode worth being loud about, so
    require_auth_credentials() now refuses to start and names the variable that's
    missing -- matching how implementation/answer.py already treats SUPABASE_DB_URL.
    Empty strings count as missing, not just unset ones.
    
    It's called from main() rather than at import time on purpose: tests/test_app.py
    imports this module to exercise the Refresh History helpers, and that shouldn't
    require the server's credentials. Launching it does.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

